 [Shell integration](prompt.md) ## The recordings
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) 
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) Each document opens with a recording of the feature actually running. They are not screencasts
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) somebody performed: every one is a script in [`scripts/demo`](../scripts/demo/), driven into a
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) real frontend by [`record.py`](../scripts/demo/record.py), so any of them can be made again
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) after the code changes — and a film that stops matching hexe is a bug in one or the other.
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) 
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) ```sh
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) make demo-fixture                      # build the deterministic world under /tmp
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) make demo-record DEMO=floats           # re-record one
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) make demo-record                       # all of them
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) make demo-publish                      # upload, remember the ids in casts.tsv
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) make demo-embed                        # put the players back in the documents
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) ```
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) 
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) They are filmed **with the author's own configuration and the author's own shell**: `fixture.sh`
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) copies `~/.config/hexe` and `~/.config/oslo` in as they are, so the prompt, the status bar, the
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) float borders and every key a film presses are the ones in daily use. Two things are changed on the
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) way in and both are marked in the fixture: the four agent floats get local, offline commands — a
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) recording must not open a paid agent or touch a network — and a small block of extra bindings is
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) appended for the actions the config does not bind but the documents describe.
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) 
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) The recorder is not tmux driving hexe, the way a shell's demos are usually made: hexe *is* the
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) thing under the recorder, and the chords these demos press are exactly the ones an outer
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) multiplexer would want for itself. Instead `record.py` opens a pty, starts a real frontend on it,
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) answers the terminal queries a TUI asks at startup — including the kitty keyboard one, without
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) which `Ctrl+Alt+.` cannot be sent at all — types, and writes what comes back as an asciicast.
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) Nothing in a film is synthesised: if a feature fails on the machine doing the recording, the film
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) shows it failing, which is what [isolation](isolation.md) is a recording of.
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) 
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) Every demo runs at 240×60 under its own instance, its own copy of the configuration, its own `HOME`
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) and its own state directories, so a recording can neither see nor disturb a real session or a real
 [Shell integration](prompt.md) history.
